mer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0206 (Translation) (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0206 (Translation) (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0206 (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0206 (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0208 (Translation) (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0208 (Translation) (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0208 (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0208 (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0210 (Translation) (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0210 (Translation) (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0210 (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0210 (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0212 (Translation) (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0212 (Translation) (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0212 (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0212 (C1712)"},{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/EWZ-52 German Citizenship Application/EWZ52-G053_0214 (C1712).tif","form":"tif","title":"EWZ52-G053_0214 (C1712)"}]}]}],"media":[{"path":"Documents/Archive/Genealogy/Zelmer/Zelmer, August (I0188)/Zelmer, August (I0188) - EWZ Pedigree, 1940 (C1697).jpg","form":"jpg","title":"Zelmer, August (I0188) - EWZ Pedigree, 1940 (C1697)","oid":"O1770"}],"notes":["Refer to C1713 for August Zelmer's health card details. Image 2436 stated that his health history included an infection of the middle year two years ago (1938). August was known as EWZ person #152768. August, in February 1940 was 179 cm or 5'-10.5\" tall and weighed 76 kg or 167.5 lbs. At this time, he was 23 years old.\n\nRefer to C1712. \n\nAccording to image 0196, he was a farm worker.  Some additional excerpts of EWZ documents are as follows:\n\nImage 0196 - August Zelmer, single, farmhand, born July 23, 1917 in Bottorf, Hanover, evang.\nCitizenship: Polish\nRacial heritage: Arian.\n\nImage 1098 - Handwritten comments on left side: Volksdeutscher, speaks fluent German, Polish and Ukrainian, went to German school in Klein Gluscha because there was none in the settlement. Parents are said to be Volksdeutche; they went ahead of him. SS logo on the notes, indicating that he was an SS soldier.\n\n\"In the wake of the Hitler-Stalin pact in 1939, there was a resettlement operation of several hundred thousand people of so-called ethnic Germans from the Soviet-occupied areas of Poland (this also included Volhynia), the Baltic states and Bessarabia.\nMost of these people arrived in Germany or areas occupied by Germany at the beginning of 1940. They were all subjected to a formal naturalization process which culminated in the naturalization certificate. After that they were so-called “Reich Germans” or “Loot Germans” and also “Aryans”. It was very important for an SS man to have documents that identified him as an Aryan.\"\n- https://www.forum-der-wehrmacht.de/index.php?thread/44410-7-14-ss-t-i-regt-weimar-buchenwald/&postID=467715#post467715\n\nImage 0202 - Presentation of the Naturalization Document of SS Man August Zelmer 7/14 SS-T.1 Regt. in Weimar-Buchenwald. August was an SS reserve soldier (SS-Mann der Reserve) in the 7.Sturm (later 7.Kompanie) of the 14.SS-T.Standarte. The 7th Sturm was a rifle unit, i.e. infantry.\n\nImage 0204 - Receipt: Today I received the Naturalization Document No.l 152768/L, issued by the Special Envoy of the Minister of the Interior on February 18, 1940 in Lodz. Dated Weimar, June 24,1940.\n\nImage 0206 - Refer to letter written and signed by August Zelmer requesting the immediate return of his repatriation ID. #0208: Attests to the fact that August \"has obtained his German Citizenship at the moment this document is presented to him.  The naturalization does not apply to family members.\" \n\nImage 0210 - Occupation: farmhand, Residence: Podborocze, Hair: dark blond, Eyes: blue, Distinguishing marks: none, Issued October 1938."],"changed":"1 NOV 2023"}